      "problem": "$|-\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}|=$",
      "worked_solution": "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|4{c}^{2}|}{|7|} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{|7|} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${c}^{2}\\ge 0$ for any real $c,$ we have that $|{c}^{2}|={c}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{7} & =\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
